59
00:03:57,392 --> 00:04:01,153
participate. Confirm any local
arrangements with your delivery team.

60
00:04:02,681 --> 00:04:07,454
Turn to page four of your Participant
Guide. Bring two or three students to

61
00:04:07,455 --> 00:04:12,421
mind. Choose students whose experiences
differ in some way: perhaps their age,

62
00:04:12,422 --> 00:04:17,389
how they communicate, or what different
parts of school are like for them. Use

63
00:04:17,390 --> 00:04:21,905
initials or a role description in your
notes. We won't need identifying

64
00:04:21,906 --> 00:04:27,001
information. Begin with the whole person.
What interests them? What strengths do

65
00:04:27,002 --> 00:04:31,711
you notice? How do they connect with
people or with the things they enjoy?

66
00:04:31,712 --> 00:04:36,678
Then ask yourself: what am I here to
understand more deeply about their school

67
00:04:36,679 --> 00:04:41,388
experience? Keep that as an open question,
rather than deciding the answer

68
00:04:41,389 --> 00:04:46,033
now. This is private thinking first.
Sharing is optional. We'll return to

69
00:04:46,034 --> 00:04:50,936
these same students when we map the school
day, consider learning design, and

70
00:04:50,937 --> 00:04:56,032
reflect on what to carry forward. Give
yourself a moment to choose your students

71
00:04:56,033 --> 00:04:57,386
and make a few notes.

72
00:05:05,401 --> 00:05:10,123
Keep those notes available. You don't need
a complete picture yet. The gaps in

73
00:05:10,124 --> 00:05:14,478
what we know are part of the work. As we
move through the workshop, keep

74
00:05:14,479 --> 00:05:18,894
distinguishing what a student has
communicated, what you've observed, and

75
00:05:18,895 --> 00:05:21,652
what you'd still like to understand with
them.

76
00:05:23,161 --> 00:05:27,917
We're going to begin with student voices.
Listen to each speaker's own account, and

77
00:05:27,918 --> 00:05:32,501
allow it to stand on its own terms. You
don't need to diagnose, compare speakers

78
00:05:32,502 --> 00:05:36,794
with students you know, or reduce the
video to one lesson. Just notice what

79
00:05:36,795 --> 00:05:41,146
stays with you, and what you want to
understand more deeply. We'll have time

80
00:05:41,147 --> 00:05:42,710
to reflect after the video.

81
00:08:05,121 --> 00:08:09,021
Take a moment before moving into
discussion. Keep the speakers' own words

82
00:08:09,022 --> 00:08:11,620
and experiences at the centre of your
reflection.

83
00:08:14,641 --> 00:08:19,530
Stay on page four of your Participant
Guide. What did you notice? What has

84
00:08:19,531 --> 00:08:24,554
stayed with you? What would you like to
understand more deeply? Begin with a

85
00:08:24,555 --> 00:08:29,846
little private writing. Anchor your notes
in what the speakers said, rather than

86
00:08:29,847 --> 00:08:34,670
making an interpretation from their
appearance or behaviour. Then, if you

87
00:08:34,671 --> 00:08:39,627
wish, share one reflection with someone
nearby. Try to leave each speaker's

88
00:08:39,628 --> 00:08:44,852
account intact. We don't need to reach
agreement about a single message, and we

89
00:08:44,853 --> 00:08:49,742
don't need to compare a speaker with one
particular child. Make room for a

90
00:08:49,743 --> 00:08:54,699
question that remains open as well as
something that feels clear. Take some

91
00:08:54,700 --> 00:08:56,575
time with those prompts now.

92
00:09:04,601 --> 00:09:09,417
Let's return. Keep one question from that
reflection where you can see it. We will

93
00:09:09,418 --> 00:09:13,580
come back to sensory experience, thinking,
communication and the school

94
00:09:13,581 --> 00:09:18,100
day. Some questions may need further
conversation through the program or your

95
00:09:18,101 --> 00:09:22,501
local channels. Listening carefully
includes recognising what we still need

96
00:09:22,502 --> 00:09:23,036
to learn.

97
00:09:24,560 --> 00:09:29,208
Let's take a moment with the language on
this slide, because it gives us a shared

98
00:09:29,208 --> 00:09:33,448
starting point. Neurodiversity is the
whole picture. It includes everyone.

99
00:09:33,448 --> 00:09:38,154
Neurotypical and neurodivergent describe
brain patterns within that diversity, and

100
00:09:38,154 --> 00:09:42,511
autism is one expression of it. Here,
neurotypical describes the more common

101
00:09:42,511 --> 00:09:46,984
brain patterns. Neurodivergent describes
brain patterns that differ from those

102
00:09:46,984 --> 00:09:51,400
most common patterns. So both sit within
the wider picture of neurodiversity.

103
00:09:51,400 --> 00:09:55,757
Keeping those ideas clear helps us use the
language consistently through the

104
00:09:55,757 --> 00:10:00,230
day. Now, hold in mind the students you
were thinking about earlier. The words

105
00:10:00,230 --> 00:10:04,761
can help us ask better questions about
their experience, but they won't tell us

106
00:10:04,761 --> 00:10:08,770
everything about any one of them. We still
need to know that student's

107
00:10:08,770 --> 00:10:13,243
interests, their ways of communicating,
and what different parts of school are

108
00:10:13,243 --> 00:10:17,658
like for them. We'll also respect the
language people use for themselves. The

109
00:10:17,658 --> 00:10:22,074
purpose here is to support dignity and
curiosity. As we go, keep asking: does

110
00:10:22,074 --> 00:10:25,441
this language help me understand the
person in front of me?

111
00:10:26,960 --> 00:10:31,645
That brings us to what it means to be
neurodiversity affirming. The starting

112
00:10:31,646 --> 00:10:36,019
point is that differences are not
deficits. There are different ways of

113
00:10:36,020 --> 00:10:40,893
knowing, doing, being and belonging, and
everyone has strengths and experiences

114
00:10:40,894 --> 00:10:45,579
challenges. That means allowing for
different ways of thinking, learning and

115
00:10:45,580 --> 00:10:50,327
taking part. When we look at belonging,
for instance, we can stay open to the

116
00:10:50,328 --> 00:10:55,263
ways a student experiences connection,
rather than expecting it to look the same

117
00:10:55,264 --> 00:11:00,075
for everyone. In a classroom, it's easy
for the things that need our immediate

118
00:11:00,076 --> 00:11:04,948
attention to fill the whole picture. A
difficult transition. A task that hasn't

119
00:11:04,949 --> 00:11:10,009
started. A moment when joining the group
has become too much. Those things matter.

120
00:11:10,010 --> 00:11:14,883
We need to understand them and provide
support. And we need to keep the rest of

121
00:11:14,884 --> 00:11:19,444
the person visible. What interests them?
What brings them joy? How do they

122
00:11:19,445 --> 00:11:23,818
connect? What can they do that we might
miss if we're only watching for

123
00:11:23,819 --> 00:11:28,567
difficulty? Take one of your students now.
What becomes more visible when you

124
00:11:28,568 --> 00:11:33,628
include their strengths and interests in
the picture? Just hold that thought for a

125
00:11:33,629 --> 00:11:34,065
moment.

126
00:11:42,080 --> 00:11:47,321
A strengths-based approach also takes
difficulty seriously. A student can have

127
00:11:47,322 --> 00:11:52,768
real strengths and still need substantial
support. Their capacity can change with

128
00:11:52,769 --> 00:11:58,351
the task, the environment and the day. So
our language needs to make room for both.

129
00:11:58,352 --> 00:12:03,116
We can describe what's becoming difficult,
notice what the situation is

130
00:12:03,117 --> 00:12:08,359
asking of the student, and consider what
support would help. We begin with the

131
00:12:08,360 --> 00:12:11,150
person, and we stay curious about access.

132
00:12:12,680 --> 00:12:17,339
The social model helps us put that
curiosity to work. On this slide, we're

133
00:12:17,340 --> 00:12:22,446
using an environment-first lens. We look
for barriers in the place, the task, the

134
00:12:22,447 --> 00:12:27,298
routine and the expectations. Then we ask
what can change around the student.

135
00:12:27,299 --> 00:12:32,213
Here's an imaginary classroom example. A
class is gathering on the floor for a

136
00:12:32,214 --> 00:12:37,128
story. The usual expectation is that
everyone sits close together, in the same

137
00:12:37,129 --> 00:12:42,299
space, for the whole reading. One student
finds that arrangement difficult. Before

138
00:12:42,300 --> 00:12:47,023
deciding what the student needs to do
differently, we can look at what this

139
00:12:47,024 --> 00:12:52,194
routine asks of them. How crowded is the
space? What choices are there about where

140
00:12:52,195 --> 00:12:56,790
to sit? Is sitting in that particular
place necessary to take part in the

141
00:12:56,791 --> 00:13:01,961
story? We might explore another place to
sit, or a different way of joining. Those

142
00:13:01,962 --> 00:13:07,196
are possibilities to investigate with the
student. We haven't established what will

143
00:13:07,197 --> 00:13:12,111
help just by coming up with an option. The
next two steps on the slide matter:

144
00:13:12,112 --> 00:13:17,218
include the student's communication and
experience, and review whether the change

145
00:13:17,219 --> 00:13:22,325
makes participation more workable. What
can the student tell or show us about the

146
00:13:22,326 --> 00:13:27,240
change? Is it helping them take part?
Changing an environment won't make every

147
00:13:27,241 --> 00:13:31,900
difficulty disappear. It gives us
somewhere useful to begin, with barriers

148
00:13:31,901 --> 00:13:35,156
and supports that we can notice, discuss
and review.

149
00:13:36,680 --> 00:13:41,125
Let's widen that question to school
itself. You can use the space on page

150
00:13:41,126 --> 00:13:45,879
five of your Participant Guide as you
think. What is school for? Who is school

151
00:13:45,880 --> 00:13:50,201
for? How do students learn? And whose ways
of communicating, moving and

152
00:13:50,202 --> 00:13:55,017
participating are treated as expected?
These are big questions. There isn't one

153
00:13:55,018 --> 00:13:59,895
correct answer we're trying to arrive at.
They're an invitation to look again at

154
00:13:59,896 --> 00:14:04,464
familiar routines and the assumptions
inside them. Think about a routine in

155
00:14:04,465 --> 00:14:09,342
your classroom: perhaps arriving in the
morning, joining a group or showing what

156
00:14:09,343 --> 00:14:13,973
you've learnt. What does that routine seem
to assume about how students will

157
00:14:13,974 --> 00:14:18,481
communicate or participate? What do we
notice and encourage? What might be

158
00:14:18,482 --> 00:14:23,112
harder to see? This is a chance to examine
the routine with curiosity. Every

159
00:14:23,113 --> 00:14:27,989
classroom has patterns that have become
familiar. Looking at them again can open

160
00:14:27,990 --> 00:14:32,744
up choices. We can value the care and
effort that educators bring, while still

161
00:14:32,745 --> 00:14:37,190
asking whether a familiar expectation is
helping every student access the

162
00:14:37,191 --> 00:14:41,573
learning. For a moment, work quietly on
your own. Choose one of the four

163
00:14:41,574 --> 00:14:46,143
questions that catches your attention, and
jot down a thought or a question

164
00:14:46,144 --> 00:14:47,500
you'd like to explore.

165
00:14:55,520 --> 00:15:00,213
Hold on to the question you've chosen. Our
assumptions influence what we notice,

166
00:15:00,214 --> 00:15:04,609
what we reward and what we think needs to
change. We'll now take that wider

167
00:15:04,610 --> 00:15:09,184
reflection into one specific routine, so
the question becomes something we can

168
00:15:09,185 --> 00:15:10,194
examine together.

169
00:15:11,720 --> 00:15:16,942
Now turn to a small group near you. To
begin, take the question about whose ways

170
00:15:16,943 --> 00:15:21,306
of communicating, moving and participating
are treated as expected.

171
00:15:21,307 --> 00:15:26,330
Choose one familiar routine or decision to
explore together. Keep it specific

172
00:15:26,331 --> 00:15:31,487
enough to picture. You might choose how
children join a whole-class discussion,

173
00:15:31,488 --> 00:15:36,446
for example. Talk about the routine and
the system, without naming students,

174
00:15:36,447 --> 00:15:41,735
colleagues or schools. Then work through
the prompts on screen. Which assumptions

175
00:15:41,736 --> 00:15:46,495
shape that routine? Who can access it
easily? Where might the environment

176
00:15:46,496 --> 00:15:51,387
create a barrier? How could curiosity
change the next response? If you find

177
00:15:51,388 --> 00:15:56,081
yourselves focusing mainly on what a
student does, come back to what the

178
00:15:56,082 --> 00:16:00,577
environment, task or expectation is
contributing. Before we come back

179
00:16:00,578 --> 00:16:05,337
together, choose one concise observation
to share, and one example of how

180
00:16:05,338 --> 00:16:10,164
curiosity could change your next response.
Keep the prompts in view as you

181
00:16:10,165 --> 00:16:11,486
talk. You can begin.

182
00:16:19,520 --> 00:16:24,286
Let's come back together. Finish the
thought you're on, and keep your notes.

183
00:16:24,287 --> 00:16:29,053
If you're sharing with the group, offer
one concise observation: one example

184
00:16:29,054 --> 00:16:34,074
where curiosity could change your next
response. Keep the example focused on the

185
00:16:34,075 --> 00:16:39,095
routine, the task or the environment. As
we close, keep that question about your

186
00:16:39,096 --> 00:16:43,862
next response with you. The shift we're
practising is from assumptions about

187
00:16:43,863 --> 00:16:48,438
students towards questions about access,
environment and learning design.

188
00:16:48,439 --> 00:16:53,078
Curiosity gives us time to look at the
task and the setting before placing

189
00:16:53,079 --> 00:16:58,099
another demand on a student. It helps us
identify something to explore, with the

190
00:16:58,100 --> 00:17:03,120
student's experience still at the centre.
Keep your example available as we move

191
00:17:03,121 --> 00:17:03,311
on.

192
00:17:04,840 --> 00:17:10,302
The Disability Standards for Education
2005 are part of the context for our work

193
00:17:10,303 --> 00:17:15,626
on access and adjustments. There are three
Australian Government Department of

194
00:17:15,627 --> 00:17:20,535
Education resources to be aware of
alongside this workshop. The first is

195
00:17:20,536 --> 00:17:26,067
Getting started. The second is Advocating
with and for your child: A workbook for

196
00:17:26,068 --> 00:17:31,391
parents and carers. That workbook is
written for families, so it is useful for

197
00:17:31,392 --> 00:17:36,923
educators to know what parents and carers
may be reading and working through. The

198
00:17:36,924 --> 00:17:41,902
third resource is Milestones and
transitions. The links are listed in the

199
00:17:41,903 --> 00:17:47,503
facilitator resources. Use the published
materials to follow up, and take detailed

200
00:17:47,504 --> 00:17:53,103
or school-specific questions through the
appropriate program or local channel. For

201
00:17:53,104 --> 00:17:58,151
our work today, keep the connection with
access in view: understanding the

202
00:17:58,152 --> 00:18:03,268
student, looking closely at the demands of
the setting, and considering the

203
00:18:03,269 --> 00:18:07,486
adjustments and support that make
participation more workable.

204
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:14,107
Autism can be understood as difference and
as a dynamic disability. Holding both

205
00:18:14,108 --> 00:18:19,020
ideas matters. Recognising difference and
strengths does not mean overlooking

206
00:18:19,021 --> 00:18:23,870
difficulty or the need for substantial
support. Access and support needs can

207
00:18:23,871 --> 00:18:28,913
change with the task, the environment,
relationships and the capacity available

208
00:18:28,914 --> 00:18:34,150
at that time. Something a student can do
in one setting, or on one day, may not be

209
00:18:34,151 --> 00:18:39,257
available in another. We need to look at
what is happening around the student as

210
00:18:39,258 --> 00:18:44,494
well as what the task is asking of them.
That takes us away from a fixed judgement

211
00:18:44,495 --> 00:18:49,343
about what someone can always do. It
invites a more useful question: what is

212
00:18:49,344 --> 00:18:54,192
making participation possible here, and
what support is needed now? Keep the

213
00:18:54,193 --> 00:18:59,041
individual in view. There isn't one
pattern that will explain every Autistic

214
00:18:59,042 --> 00:19:03,761
student's experience. Strengths,
difficulties and fluctuating capacity all

215
00:19:03,762 --> 00:19:08,675
belong in the picture we build with the
student and the people who know them.

216
00:19:10,200 --> 00:19:15,251
Here is where the day is taking us. We'll
develop a neurodiversity affirming

217
00:19:15,252 --> 00:19:20,168
approach, deepen our understanding of
Autistic experience, notice how that

218
00:19:20,169 --> 00:19:25,422
experience meets the school day, consider
accessible learning environments, and

219
00:19:25,423 --> 00:19:30,407
work in partnership. The first two
sessions build our shared understanding.

220
00:19:30,408 --> 00:19:35,324
Then we'll bring that understanding into
the rhythms of a school day, with

221
00:19:35,325 --> 00:19:40,376
examples for primary and secondary
settings and into practical choices about

222
00:19:40,377 --> 00:19:45,428
design and support. Belonging, capacity
and joy stay with us throughout. The

223
00:19:45,429 --> 00:19:50,816
workshop schedule allows for morning tea
from ten forty-five to eleven, and lunch

224
00:19:50,817 --> 00:19:55,868
from twelve thirty to one. Treat those as
the planned timings and follow the

225
00:19:55,869 --> 00:20:01,055
arrangements for your session. Before we
move on, turn to My starting point on

226
00:20:01,056 --> 00:20:06,511
page five of your Participant Guide. Make
a brief note of where you are beginning:

227
00:20:06,512 --> 00:20:11,832
a question, a hope or an idea you want to
examine. We will return to this in our

228
00:20:11,833 --> 00:20:14,796
final reflection. Take a moment to write
now.

229
00:20:22,800 --> 00:20:27,222
Keep that starting point. You don't have
to resolve it yet. Next we'll explore

230
00:20:27,223 --> 00:20:31,818
sensory experience, thinking and internal
signals, using the students you have in

231
00:20:31,819 --> 00:20:34,920
mind to connect the ideas with your own
school context.

232
00:20:41,440 --> 00:20:46,472
Let's return to the students you brought
to mind. In this session we'll explore

233
00:20:46,473 --> 00:20:51,312
sensory processing, Autistic thinking and
internal signals. You can find the

234
00:20:51,313 --> 00:20:55,893
session summary on page six of your
Participant Guide. We are building a

235
00:20:55,894 --> 00:21:00,797
fuller picture of each student. These
ideas give us areas for curiosity; they

236
00:21:00,798 --> 00:21:05,572
are not a checklist for deciding what a
student must be like. Keep noticing

237
00:21:05,573 --> 00:21:10,670
strengths and interests, the environment,
the demands of a task, and the support

238
00:21:10,671 --> 00:21:15,638
that may help. We'll look at these areas
separately so we can understand them,

239
00:21:15,639 --> 00:21:20,478
then begin putting them together again. In
the next session we'll carry that

240
00:21:20,479 --> 00:21:25,059
understanding into communication,
connection and the everyday rhythms of

241
00:21:25,060 --> 00:21:25,511
school.

242
00:21:27,120 --> 00:21:31,914
We'll start with sensory experience: how
the world arrives. Before we ask a

243
00:21:31,915 --> 00:21:36,839
student to join a conversation, begin a
task or make sense of an instruction,

244
00:21:36,840 --> 00:21:41,829
there is already a sensory environment
around them. That environment can bring

245
00:21:41,830 --> 00:21:46,690
pleasure, interest and connection, as well
as difficulty. Keep both in view.

246
00:21:46,691 --> 00:21:51,485
We're interested in sensory joy and
authentic expression alongside overload

247
00:21:51,486 --> 00:21:56,540
and barriers. As we work, remember that an
observation can open a question, but

248
00:21:56,541 --> 00:22:01,335
it cannot tell us everything about someone
else's internal experience. Stay

249
00:22:01,336 --> 00:22:03,992
curious about the person and the context.

250
00:22:05,520 --> 00:22:10,053
What do you understand by sensory
processing? Before we settle on a shared

251
00:22:10,054 --> 00:22:15,023
definition, take a moment to put it into
your own words. Think about what happens

252
00:22:15,024 --> 00:22:20,054
between information arriving through the
senses and the response you might notice.

253
00:22:28,080 --> 00:22:33,692
For this workshop, sensory processing is
the way the brain receives, processes and

254
00:22:33,693 --> 00:22:39,167
responds to information from the senses.
There are three parts to hold together:

255
00:22:39,168 --> 00:22:44,226
receiving information, making sense of it,
and responding. We'll look more

256
00:22:44,227 --> 00:22:49,355
closely at that sequence in a moment.
First, let's widen the picture of the

257
00:22:49,356 --> 00:22:50,602
senses themselves.

258
00:22:52,120 --> 00:22:57,265
Many of us learnt five senses: touch,
vision, taste, hearing and smell. This

259
00:22:57,266 --> 00:23:02,480
workshop uses eight. The other three are
proprioception, vestibular sense and

260
00:23:02,481 --> 00:23:08,037
interoception. Touch includes information
about texture, pressure and temperature.

261
00:23:08,038 --> 00:23:12,772
Vision gives information about things such
as colour, shape, depth and

262
00:23:12,773 --> 00:23:18,192
position. Gustatory and oral experience
includes taste, texture, temperature and

263
00:23:18,193 --> 00:23:23,064
the sensation of chewing. Hearing includes
pitch, loudness, distance and

264
00:23:23,065 --> 00:23:27,455
familiar sounds. Smell can also be closely
connected with memory.

265
00:23:27,456 --> 00:23:32,875
Proprioception gives us information about
where our body is in space, drawing on

266
00:23:32,876 --> 00:23:38,021
signals from our muscles and joints.
Pressure or movement may be something a

267
00:23:38,022 --> 00:23:42,962
student seeks for that input. The
vestibular sense relates to balance and

268
00:23:42,963 --> 00:23:47,833
movement, including the speed and
direction of head movement. Rocking or

269
00:23:47,834 --> 00:23:53,254
spinning may serve a regulating purpose
for some students. Interoception relates

270
00:23:53,255 --> 00:23:58,605
to signals from inside the body. We'll
return to it later in the session. These

271
00:23:58,606 --> 00:24:04,026
senses work together. A single classroom
experience can involve several at once.

272
00:24:04,027 --> 00:24:08,897
The three less familiar senses can be
especially useful to consider when

273
00:24:08,898 --> 00:24:13,975
something about a student's experience
isn't yet clear to us. Keep these as

274
00:24:13,976 --> 00:24:19,121
possibilities to explore, rather than
explanations we can assign by watching

275
00:24:19,122 --> 00:24:24,130
alone. Students will differ. There is also
an interoception webinar in the

276
00:24:24,131 --> 00:24:27,970
Positive Partnerships resources if you
want to follow up.

277
00:24:29,480 --> 00:24:33,678
Here is the sequence again: sensory
information, processing, response.

278
00:24:33,679 --> 00:24:37,694
Imagine you're wearing a jumper and
becoming hot. Information about

279
00:24:37,695 --> 00:24:42,501
temperature arrives. You make sense of
that information, including what it means

280
00:24:42,502 --> 00:24:47,247
in relation to your experience, and you
might respond by taking the jumper off.

281
00:24:47,248 --> 00:24:51,385
In a classroom, this processing helps us
keep track of our bodies and

282
00:24:51,386 --> 00:24:56,010
surroundings, notice what matters and
direct attention. It is part of safety,

283
00:24:56,011 --> 00:25:00,939
learning and engagement. There is a great
deal happening before a response becomes

284
00:25:00,940 --> 00:25:05,746
visible to someone else. That is why we
need to look beyond the behaviour we can

285
00:25:05,747 --> 00:25:10,492
see. Sensory processing is not simply a
deliberate choice a student makes about

286
00:25:10,493 --> 00:25:14,691
how the world affects them. When we notice
a response, we can ask what

287
00:25:14,692 --> 00:25:19,194
information is arriving, what the
environment is asking of the student, and

288
00:25:19,195 --> 00:25:21,445
what we need to understand with them.

289
00:25:22,960 --> 00:25:27,952
We're going to watch two short clips. The
first is an animation that explores

290
00:25:27,953 --> 00:25:33,011
sensory overwhelm through images and
sound. The next offers a personal account

291
00:25:33,012 --> 00:25:37,807
of sensory joy. Both belong in our
understanding of sensory experience. As

292
00:25:37,808 --> 00:25:42,471
you watch the animation, notice what it
draws your attention to. You may

293
00:25:42,472 --> 00:25:47,595
recognise something, or you may encounter
a portrayal that is unfamiliar. Leave

294
00:25:47,596 --> 00:25:52,588
room for either response, and remember
that no single portrayal can represent

295
00:25:52,589 --> 00:25:57,581
every Autistic person's experience. We'll
leave a quiet moment after the clip

296
00:25:57,582 --> 00:25:58,764
before continuing.

297
00:28:26,000 --> 00:28:30,961
Take a moment with what you've seen and
heard. The animation gives us a way to

298
00:28:30,962 --> 00:28:35,858
consider sensory overwhelm, while each
person's experience remains their own.

299
00:28:35,859 --> 00:28:40,627
Keep any questions it raises with you.
We'll now make room for sensory joy.

300
00:28:43,640 --> 00:28:49,180
In this next clip, we'll hear a personal
account of sensory joy. As you listen,

301
00:28:49,181 --> 00:28:54,366
consider what becomes visible when we
include pleasure, connection and the

302
00:28:54,367 --> 00:28:57,989
richness of sensory experience in our
understanding.

303
00:29:47,480 --> 00:29:53,237
Sensory experience can include intensity,
depth and pleasure as well as difficulty.

304
00:29:53,238 --> 00:29:58,785
The same person can experience both joy
and overwhelm. For example, music may be

305
00:29:58,786 --> 00:30:03,912
a source of enjoyment for someone, while a
different sound or situation is

306
00:30:03,913 --> 00:30:08,757
difficult. That is an illustration, rather
than a conclusion about any

307
00:30:08,758 --> 00:30:14,024
individual speaker. Keep asking what
brings this particular student joy, and

308
00:30:14,025 --> 00:30:16,271
how school can make room for it.

309
00:30:17,800 --> 00:30:22,855
Sensory joy and stimming belong in this
conversation. Stimming can be part of

310
00:30:22,856 --> 00:30:27,645
regulation, pleasure, connection and
authentic expression. Our task is to

311
00:30:27,646 --> 00:30:33,033
understand what it means for the student,
rather than treating it as disruption by

312
00:30:33,034 --> 00:30:37,224
default. Notice what brings joy. Recognise
the student's ways of

313
00:30:37,225 --> 00:30:42,280
regulating and expressing themselves.
Avoid making harmless stimming a target

314
00:30:42,281 --> 00:30:47,070
simply because it looks different from
what is expected. And ask what the

315
00:30:47,071 --> 00:30:51,993
student would like other people to
understand. Think about one student now.

316
00:30:51,994 --> 00:30:56,982
Where is sensory joy visible in their
school day? What opportunities do they

317
00:30:56,983 --> 00:31:01,905
have to enjoy, explore or regulate in ways
that work for them? Make a brief

318
00:31:01,906 --> 00:31:06,096
note of something you know, and something
you would like to ask.

319
00:31:14,120 --> 00:31:18,652
Keep those two things distinct: what you
already know from the student, and what

320
00:31:18,653 --> 00:31:22,611
you still need to explore. Sensory support
is about more than reducing

321
00:31:22,612 --> 00:31:26,799
difficulty. It can also make space for
pleasure and for ways of being that

322
00:31:26,800 --> 00:31:28,004
matter to the person.

323
00:31:29,520 --> 00:31:34,006
This slide introduces four sensory
response patterns from occupational

324
00:31:34,007 --> 00:31:38,622
therapist Winnie Dunn's framework. You may
encounter this language in an

325
00:31:38,623 --> 00:31:43,759
occupational therapy report or a Sensory
Profile School Companion questionnaire.

326
00:31:43,760 --> 00:31:48,831
The patterns are useful lenses, rather
than fixed types of person. Registration

327
00:31:48,832 --> 00:31:53,772
describes a pattern of noticing less
sensory information or missing cues that

328
00:31:53,773 --> 00:31:58,974
others notice. Seeking describes actively
looking for more input, perhaps through

329
00:31:58,975 --> 00:32:03,981
movement, touch or sound. Sensitivity
describes noticing sensory input readily

330
00:32:03,982 --> 00:32:08,663
and being affected by it, without
necessarily taking action to reduce it.

331
00:32:08,664 --> 00:32:13,734
Avoiding describes actively trying to
reduce input, perhaps by moving away from

332
00:32:13,735 --> 00:32:18,221
a crowd or covering the ears. The slide
also uses under-responsive and

333
00:32:18,222 --> 00:32:22,773
over-responsive language, and
distinguishes more passive responses from

334
00:32:22,774 --> 00:32:27,454
active ways of changing the input. Keep
that distinction connected to the

335
00:32:27,455 --> 00:32:32,006
student's experience, rather than treating
the labels as a judgement. A

336
00:32:32,007 --> 00:32:36,948
student may show different patterns for
different senses. Their responses may

337
00:32:36,949 --> 00:32:41,369
also change with the context, the load and
the day. We are looking at

338
00:32:41,370 --> 00:32:46,506
nervous-system responses, not simply a
decision to cooperate or avoid a task. So

339
00:32:46,507 --> 00:32:51,123
use the framework to sharpen your
questions. What input is present? What

340
00:32:51,124 --> 00:32:55,674
has the student communicated? What pattern
might be worth exploring? An

341
00:32:55,675 --> 00:33:00,551
observation gives us a starting point, not
certainty about what is happening

342
00:33:00,552 --> 00:33:01,006
inside.

343
00:33:02,520 --> 00:33:07,748
Let's apply that to a classroom routine.
Turn to the sensory activity on page

344
00:33:07,749 --> 00:33:13,115
seven of your Participant Guide, and work
in a small group. Choose one familiar

345
00:33:13,116 --> 00:33:18,275
routine that you can describe without
identifying a student or school. Start

346
00:33:18,276 --> 00:33:22,610
with the sensory demands of the
environment and the task, before

347
00:33:22,611 --> 00:33:27,908
discussing a student's response. What
might someone need to take in, filter or

348
00:33:27,909 --> 00:33:32,242
manage? You could consider sound,
lighting, movement, proximity,

349
00:33:32,243 --> 00:33:37,747
transitions or materials. These are areas
to investigate, not a universal list of

350
00:33:37,748 --> 00:33:43,113
triggers. Next, consider possible ways to
offer access or choice. How could the

351
00:33:43,114 --> 00:33:48,618
student communicate what works for them?
Treat each idea as a question to explore

352
00:33:48,619 --> 00:33:54,191
with the student, rather than a solution
you've already established. This is not a

353
00:33:54,192 --> 00:33:59,213
simulation of being Autistic. It is a
review of what the routine asks of a

354
00:33:59,214 --> 00:34:04,236
person. Choose the routine, map its
demands, then identify an option and a

355
00:34:04,237 --> 00:34:07,813
question you want to investigate. Begin
together now.

356
00:34:15,840 --> 00:34:20,956
Let's come back. Look at the option and
question your group has recorded. What

357
00:34:20,957 --> 00:34:25,741
could you change, offer or investigate
before increasing the demands on a

358
00:34:25,742 --> 00:34:30,526
student? Keep the sequence in mind:
examine the environment and the task,

359
00:34:30,527 --> 00:34:35,843
include the student's communication, and
review what actually helps. The point is

360
00:34:35,844 --> 00:34:41,160
to develop a more precise question about
access, with room for different students

361
00:34:41,161 --> 00:34:42,820
to need different things.

362
00:34:44,360 --> 00:34:48,980
We've been considering how the world
arrives through the senses. Now we'll

363
00:34:48,981 --> 00:34:53,284
turn to how the mind works with
information and where attention goes.

364
00:34:53,285 --> 00:34:58,284
Keep your students in mind as we explore
detail-first processing, deep focus and

365
00:34:58,285 --> 00:35:03,158
executive functioning. These concepts
won't describe every Autistic student in

366
00:35:03,159 --> 00:35:07,715
the same way. Use them to open inquiry,
and keep connecting them with the

367
00:35:07,716 --> 00:35:09,993
particular person, task and context.

368
00:35:11,520 --> 00:35:16,892
A detail-first approach offers one way of
understanding Autistic thinking. Top-down

369
00:35:16,893 --> 00:35:21,937
processing uses expectations and the wider
picture to help filter information.

370
00:35:21,938 --> 00:35:26,851
Bottom-up processing begins with the
details and builds towards the whole. A

371
00:35:26,852 --> 00:35:32,223
detail-first account places more emphasis
on that detailed information arriving and

372
00:35:32,224 --> 00:35:37,334
being worked through. That can help us
consider both depth and load. Noticing a

373
00:35:37,335 --> 00:35:42,706
change, an inconsistency or a pattern can
be a strength. Processing a great deal of

374
00:35:42,707 --> 00:35:47,293
detail can also make a situation
demanding. Neither conclusion tells us

375
00:35:47,294 --> 00:35:52,338
exactly what a particular student is
experiencing. The strengths on this slide

376
00:35:52,339 --> 00:35:57,383
include pattern recognition, systems
thinking, deep memory, logical precision,

377
00:35:57,384 --> 00:36:02,100
honesty and directness, depth of
connection, and intense engagement. Take

378
00:36:02,101 --> 00:36:07,342
each seriously as something valuable in
itself. Strengths are part of a student's

379
00:36:07,343 --> 00:36:12,190
present identity and learning, rather than
compensation for a difficulty or

380
00:36:12,191 --> 00:36:16,514
only future potential. You might recognise
connections with sensory

381
00:36:16,515 --> 00:36:21,101
intensity, literal language or deep focus.
Keep these as connections to

382
00:36:21,102 --> 00:36:26,146
explore, rather than assuming a thinking
process from behaviour alone. Turn to

383
00:36:26,147 --> 00:36:31,387
Strengths I can see on page eight of your
Participant Guide. For the students you

384
00:36:31,388 --> 00:36:36,236
have in mind, note the strengths you
recognise. What evidence supports that

385
00:36:36,237 --> 00:36:41,215
understanding? What might you be missing
if your attention is mainly drawn to

386
00:36:41,216 --> 00:36:41,936
difficulty?

387
00:36:49,960 --> 00:36:54,493
Keep those strengths visible as we
continue. They can help us recognise what

388
00:36:54,494 --> 00:36:58,483
a student brings to learning and
connection, while still taking the

389
00:36:58,484 --> 00:37:00,176
support they need seriously.

390
00:37:01,680 --> 00:37:06,236
Think for a moment about your own
attention. Does it move across many

391
00:37:06,237 --> 00:37:11,128
topics, or settle deeply into one? That
question introduces monotropism: a

392
00:37:11,129 --> 00:37:16,356
framework for understanding attention
focused deeply on fewer things at a time.

393
00:37:16,357 --> 00:37:21,583
Dinah Murray, Mike Lesser and Wenn Lawson
developed the framework, and Autistic

394
00:37:21,584 --> 00:37:26,676
community voices have continued to extend
it. One image is a torch beam and a

395
00:37:26,677 --> 00:37:32,171
floodlight. A floodlight spreads across a
wide area; a torch illuminates a narrower

396
00:37:32,172 --> 00:37:37,198
area intensely. Another is a river with
many channels compared with one deep

397
00:37:37,199 --> 00:37:42,090
channel. These are ways to picture
different patterns of attention, rather

398
00:37:42,091 --> 00:37:47,250
than a test for sorting people. Deep
interests can provide a strong route into

399
00:37:47,251 --> 00:37:52,612
learning, memory and joy. Flow, that full
absorption in an activity, is something

400
00:37:52,613 --> 00:37:57,906
worth protecting. It is more useful to
recognise that engagement than dismiss it

401
00:37:57,907 --> 00:38:03,133
as an obsession. Now picture a student
absorbed in a construction task, drawing

402
00:38:03,134 --> 00:38:08,293
or researching space. Asking them to stop
for recess may require a substantial

403
00:38:08,294 --> 00:38:12,918
shift. Tendril theory offers another
image: attention reaching into an

404
00:38:12,919 --> 00:38:18,212
activity, then needing time to withdraw
before moving elsewhere. Transitions can

405
00:38:18,213 --> 00:38:23,707
have a real cost. Warning and scaffolding
may help make that shift more manageable.

406
00:38:23,708 --> 00:38:28,465
Keep asking what support fits this student
and this moment. Detail-first

407
00:38:28,466 --> 00:38:33,291
processing helps us consider how
information arrives; deep focus helps us

408
00:38:33,292 --> 00:38:35,301
consider where attention goes.

409
00:38:36,840 --> 00:38:42,175
Executive functioning gives us another set
of questions about the demands of the

410
00:38:42,176 --> 00:38:47,443
school day. Let's name the ten areas on
the slide. Planning is working out what

411
00:38:47,444 --> 00:38:52,374
needs to happen. Organisation is arranging
materials and information. Time

412
00:38:52,375 --> 00:38:56,967
management involves judging and using
time. Working memory helps hold

413
00:38:56,968 --> 00:39:01,965
information in mind while we use it.
Metacognition means thinking about our

414
00:39:01,966 --> 00:39:06,828
own thinking. Emotional regulation
concerns managing emotional responses.

415
00:39:06,829 --> 00:39:11,894
Flexibility involves adapting when
something changes. Attention is directing

416
00:39:11,895 --> 00:39:16,689
and sustaining focus. Task initiation is
getting started. Persistence is

417
00:39:16,690 --> 00:39:22,092
continuing with a task. Now consider an
ordinary classroom activity. What does it

418
00:39:22,093 --> 00:39:27,428
ask a student to plan, start, remember,
sequence, organise, switch and complete?

419
00:39:27,429 --> 00:39:32,359
A seemingly simple instruction can contain
several demands. Interruptions,

420
00:39:32,360 --> 00:39:37,290
timing and the way instructions are
presented can change what is required.

421
00:39:37,291 --> 00:39:42,760
These terms are lenses for inquiry, not a
checklist to apply to a student. A delay

422
00:39:42,761 --> 00:39:48,164
in starting does not tell us, by itself,
that someone lacks effort or motivation.

423
00:39:48,165 --> 00:39:53,365
Useful support responds to the specific
demand in the specific context. As you

424
00:39:53,366 --> 00:39:58,768
look across the ten areas, consider where
each appears in the school day for your

425
00:39:58,769 --> 00:40:03,631
students, and where task design, timing or
support might make access more

426
00:40:03,632 --> 00:40:04,239
workable.

427
00:40:05,760 --> 00:40:10,084
Let's take getting started as one example.
The slide connects it with

428
00:40:10,085 --> 00:40:14,981
detail-first processing: starting can be
hard when the whole task is not clear

429
00:40:14,982 --> 00:40:19,624
yet. Some students may not be able to
identify the first step they need to

430
00:40:19,625 --> 00:40:23,822
take. That gives us a more useful question
than simply repeating an

431
00:40:23,823 --> 00:40:28,973
instruction to begin. Is the task clear?
Is its purpose and shape available to the

432
00:40:28,974 --> 00:40:33,743
student? Can they identify a first step?
Those questions don't establish the

433
00:40:33,744 --> 00:40:38,005
reason for a particular student's
difficulty. They give us something

434
00:40:38,006 --> 00:40:42,711
specific to check with them. Support can
then respond to the demand we have

435
00:40:42,712 --> 00:40:46,209
understood, rather than an assumption
about willingness.

436
00:40:47,720 --> 00:40:52,251
Switching focus gives us a second example.
It can be difficult for some

437
00:40:52,252 --> 00:40:57,236
students to stop what they are doing and
move their attention elsewhere. Think

438
00:40:57,237 --> 00:41:02,481
back to the student deeply absorbed in an
activity when recess begins. Transitions

439
00:41:02,482 --> 00:41:07,336
and unexpected interruptions can be
significant because attention is already

440
00:41:07,337 --> 00:41:12,516
deeply invested. The slide uses the image
of tunnel vision for the mind. It helps

441
00:41:12,517 --> 00:41:17,177
us consider the effort involved in
disengaging and redirecting attention.

442
00:41:17,178 --> 00:41:22,227
Keep the question practical: what is the
transition asking of this student, and

443
00:41:22,228 --> 00:41:27,277
what warning or scaffolding could make it
more manageable? A timetable tells us

444
00:41:27,278 --> 00:41:32,004
when the next activity starts. It doesn't,
by itself, tell us what someone

445
00:41:32,005 --> 00:41:34,205
needs in order to make that shift.

446
00:41:35,720 --> 00:41:40,425
A young person's account of deep focus and
transitions is planned for this point

447
00:41:40,426 --> 00:41:44,893
in the workshop, but the clip is not
included in the supplied materials yet.

448
00:41:44,894 --> 00:41:49,301
Hearing directly from Autistic people
remains central to this learning. The

449
00:41:49,302 --> 00:41:54,007
frameworks we've introduced can help us
form questions; they cannot stand in for

450
00:41:54,008 --> 00:41:58,296
a person's own account. Keep that
distinction as we move into discussion.

451
00:41:58,297 --> 00:42:02,645
What do you know from the students
themselves about their interests, their

452
00:42:02,646 --> 00:42:07,173
focus and the support they find useful?
Where do you still need to listen and

453
00:42:07,174 --> 00:42:07,530
learn?

454
00:42:09,040 --> 00:42:14,175
Turn to the thinking discussion on page
eight, and work with a partner. Hold your

455
00:42:14,176 --> 00:42:19,312
students in mind as you explore the four
questions on screen. What deep interests

456
00:42:19,313 --> 00:42:23,742
do they have? What do you notice when they
are fully engaged, and does

457
00:42:23,743 --> 00:42:28,686
engagement look the same for each of them?
When in the school day do they have

458
00:42:28,687 --> 00:42:33,308
trouble getting started or shifting
attention? And have you observed them

459
00:42:33,309 --> 00:42:38,252
fully absorbed in something at school?
What were they doing? Include strengths

460
00:42:38,253 --> 00:42:43,196
and flow alongside the parts that are
difficult. Separate what a task requires

461
00:42:43,197 --> 00:42:48,204
from what you observe a student doing, and
leave their internal experience open

462
00:42:48,205 --> 00:42:53,405
unless they have communicated it. If you
want to extend the discussion, choose one

463
00:42:53,406 --> 00:42:58,669
task and describe it neutrally. List what
it asks someone to notice, start, hold in

464
00:42:58,670 --> 00:43:03,613
mind, switch, sequence and complete. Mark
where the environment or instruction

465
00:43:03,614 --> 00:43:08,428
might reduce unnecessary demand, then
write one question to explore with the

466
00:43:08,429 --> 00:43:12,859
student or team. Begin with the four
questions, and leave time to note

467
00:43:12,860 --> 00:43:15,170
something you want to carry forward.

468
00:43:23,200 --> 00:43:27,811
Let's come back together. If you're
sharing, choose one brief observation or

469
00:43:27,812 --> 00:43:32,178
question from your discussion. Keep it
connected with a specific student

470
00:43:32,179 --> 00:43:37,036
experience or task, without identifying
the student. The purpose is to move from

471
00:43:37,037 --> 00:43:41,463
understanding a concept to recognising
something worth exploring in daily

472
00:43:41,464 --> 00:43:46,076
learning. Deep interests, starting,
shifting and flow can all help us notice

473
00:43:46,077 --> 00:43:50,688
more carefully. We can work with the
student's attention and strengths while

474
00:43:50,689 --> 00:43:55,608
considering the support they need. Keep
those notes for our next session, when we

475
00:43:55,609 --> 00:43:59,236
connect this thinking with communication
and the school day.

476
00:44:00,760 --> 00:44:05,373
The final part of this session turns
inward. We named interoception among the

477
00:44:05,374 --> 00:44:09,987
eight senses; now we'll return to it more
closely. What is happening inside a

478
00:44:09,988 --> 00:44:14,722
person may not be visible to an observer.
A student can be having an experience

479
00:44:14,723 --> 00:44:19,215
that we do not recognise from the surface.
Keep that in mind as we consider

480
00:44:19,216 --> 00:44:23,282
body signals, emotions and the ways we
build understanding together.

481
00:44:24,800 --> 00:44:29,751
Interoception is about understanding our
body's internal signals. It helps us

482
00:44:29,752 --> 00:44:35,095
answer the question, how am I doing? Am I
hot or cold? Am I hungry or thirsty? Do I

483
00:44:35,096 --> 00:44:39,721
need the bathroom? Am I tired? These
signals also contribute to noticing

484
00:44:39,722 --> 00:44:44,478
changes as we become upset and to
regulation. The other term on this slide

485
00:44:44,479 --> 00:44:49,365
is alexithymia: difficulty identifying and
describing emotions and feelings.

486
00:44:49,366 --> 00:44:54,383
Keep that definition clear. Identifying
what is happening and finding words to

487
00:44:54,384 --> 00:44:59,075
describe it can be difficult. The
questions on screen invite us to notice

488
00:44:59,076 --> 00:45:04,223
how much interpretation can be involved.
Am I excited or am I anxious? Is crying

489
00:45:04,224 --> 00:45:08,914
always sadness? What is my friend feeling?
We shouldn't assume the answer

490
00:45:08,915 --> 00:45:13,606
from outward behaviour. Crying alone, for
example, does not establish one

491
00:45:13,607 --> 00:45:18,559
particular feeling. And an apparently calm
surface doesn't tell us everything

492
00:45:18,560 --> 00:45:23,641
about what is happening inside. These
terms help us recognise why understanding

493
00:45:23,642 --> 00:45:28,529
internal experience matters. They are a
reason to stay open to the student's

494
00:45:28,530 --> 00:45:33,546
communication and to build understanding
together, rather than to decide their

495
00:45:33,547 --> 00:45:35,110
internal state for them.

496
00:45:36,640 --> 00:45:41,335
Sometimes a student experiences a build-up
inside, while the surface gives

497
00:45:41,336 --> 00:45:46,352
us little indication. If we wait until
distress is obvious, we may miss earlier

498
00:45:46,353 --> 00:45:51,241
opportunities to respond. The more we
understand a student's early signs, the

499
00:45:51,242 --> 00:45:55,550
better placed we are to recognise when
support might be useful. That

500
00:45:55,551 --> 00:46:00,825
understanding needs to be built with each
student, their family and other staff. No

501
00:46:00,826 --> 00:46:05,585
one person's observation gives us the
whole picture. Think of a shared map:

502
00:46:05,586 --> 00:46:10,216
what the student communicates, what
different people notice, what remains

503
00:46:10,217 --> 00:46:14,976
uncertain, and how people can respond
consistently. The map needs to remain

504
00:46:14,977 --> 00:46:19,479
open to revision as circumstances and
capacity change. An analogy or an

505
00:46:19,480 --> 00:46:23,595
observation can help start a conversation.
It doesn't replace the

506
00:46:23,596 --> 00:46:28,806
student's own communication. Keep asking
how the student can contribute, including

507
00:46:28,807 --> 00:46:33,952
in ways other than a spoken explanation,
and what the environment can do with the

508
00:46:33,953 --> 00:46:35,431
information they share.

509
00:46:36,960 --> 00:46:42,165
Turn to Reading the signs on page nine of
your Participant Guide, and work with a

510
00:46:42,166 --> 00:46:46,916
partner. For the students you have in
mind, what signs might you notice at

511
00:46:46,917 --> 00:46:51,536
school before they become overwhelmed? Are
those signs the same for each

512
00:46:51,537 --> 00:46:56,222
student? Use initials or roles in your
notes, and distinguish what you've

513
00:46:56,223 --> 00:47:01,233
observed from what you think it might
mean. Then consider what would be useful

514
00:47:01,234 --> 00:47:06,114
for other staff and families to know. What
have they noticed that could help

515
00:47:06,115 --> 00:47:11,320
you understand the picture? How could the
student communicate their capacity, and

516
00:47:11,321 --> 00:47:16,006
what could the environment do in response?
You don't need to settle on an

517
00:47:16,007 --> 00:47:20,691
explanation for every sign. Aim to
identify something useful to share and

518
00:47:20,692 --> 00:47:25,507
something you still need to explore
together. Take a few minutes with those

519
00:47:25,508 --> 00:47:26,418
questions now.

520
00:47:34,440 --> 00:47:39,189
Let's return. Keep one point that would be
useful to discuss with the student,

521
00:47:39,190 --> 00:47:43,755
their family or other staff. The signs may
differ between students, and the

522
00:47:43,756 --> 00:47:48,320
meaning of an observation needs to be
checked rather than assumed. A shared

523
00:47:48,321 --> 00:47:52,947
understanding can help people respond more
consistently and earlier. It also

524
00:47:52,948 --> 00:47:57,574
gives us a link to our later partnership
work: making room for the student's

525
00:47:57,575 --> 00:48:01,955
communication, bringing different
perspectives together, and considering

526
00:48:01,956 --> 00:48:04,114
what support the setting can offer.

527
00:48:05,640 --> 00:48:10,487
That brings us to morning tea. The
workshop schedule allows a break from ten

528
00:48:10,488 --> 00:48:15,335
forty-five to eleven; follow the timing
arranged for your session. After the

529
00:48:15,336 --> 00:48:19,989
break, we'll bring sensory experience,
thinking and internal signals into

530
00:48:19,990 --> 00:48:24,772
communication, connection and managing
daily capacity. We'll map demand and

531
00:48:24,773 --> 00:48:29,814
capacity across an ordinary school day,
keeping the same students in mind. Keep

532
00:48:29,815 --> 00:48:34,662
your notes. The strengths you've named,
the questions about routines and the

533
00:48:34,663 --> 00:48:39,122
things you still want to understand will
all be useful when we return.

534
00:48:47,160 --> 00:48:52,382
Welcome back. We’re moving now from our
shared understanding of autism into the

535
00:48:52,382 --> 00:48:57,670
experience of a school day. You’ll find
the Session Three summary on page ten of

536
00:48:57,670 --> 00:49:02,959
your Participant Guide. Bring back the two
or three students you’ve been holding

537
00:49:02,959 --> 00:49:08,315
in mind. Keep them as different people,
with different strengths, preferences and

538
00:49:08,315 --> 00:49:12,867
experiences. We’ll begin with
communication and connection, then look

539
00:49:12,867 --> 00:49:17,419
at capacity: what a day asks of each
student, what helps and what may

540
00:49:17,419 --> 00:49:22,775
accumulate over time. You’ll map a school
day, and then turn to your own position

541
00:49:22,775 --> 00:49:27,796
as an early career teacher: finding out
when you don’t have the answers, and

542
00:49:27,796 --> 00:49:33,152
knowing who to go to. If you don’t have
your own class yet, use a placement class

543
00:49:33,152 --> 00:49:38,306
or a class you know well. If you teach
secondary, think across subjects, rooms

544
00:49:38,306 --> 00:49:43,662
and teachers as well as within your own
lesson. A relief teacher can use a recent

545
00:49:43,662 --> 00:49:49,085
booking or a class they return to often.
The timetable tells us where students are

546
00:49:49,085 --> 00:49:54,240
meant to be and what happens next. It
doesn’t tell us everything about how the

547
00:49:54,240 --> 00:49:58,390
day feels or what it takes to participate.
Keep examples within

548
00:49:58,390 --> 00:50:03,746
education practice, leave out identifying
details, and stay curious about what we

549
00:50:03,746 --> 00:50:08,834
don’t yet know. Your day map and support
map will travel into the design work

550
00:50:08,834 --> 00:50:09,637
after lunch.

551
00:50:11,160 --> 00:50:16,456
Let’s begin with communication. Speech is
one way of communicating, and we’ll be

552
00:50:16,457 --> 00:50:21,418
looking at a much wider picture: how
students receive information, how they

553
00:50:21,419 --> 00:50:26,848
share what matters to them, and the ways
we make both possible. The amount someone

554
00:50:26,849 --> 00:50:31,944
speaks doesn’t tell us how much they
understand. A student may need different

555
00:50:31,945 --> 00:50:37,442
ways to understand an instruction, answer
a question, ask for help, request a pause

556
00:50:37,443 --> 00:50:42,471
or say no. All of those messages matter.
Communication access belongs in the

557
00:50:42,472 --> 00:50:47,701
design of the classroom and the school
day. We’ll talk about practical options,

558
00:50:47,702 --> 00:50:52,931
including augmentative and alternative
communication, or A A C. There isn’t one

559
00:50:52,932 --> 00:50:57,758
approach that suits every student.
Individual assessment and intervention

560
00:50:57,759 --> 00:51:02,787
questions belong with the student’s speech
pathologist or relevant team; our

561
00:51:02,788 --> 00:51:07,211
focus here is what educators can notice,
make available and change.

562
00:51:08,721 --> 00:51:13,943
Let’s start with a quick, quiet think.
Since you woke up this morning, how have

563
00:51:13,944 --> 00:51:19,368
you communicated? See if you can think of
three different ways. Perhaps you spoke,

564
00:51:19,369 --> 00:51:24,457
sent a message, pointed, showed someone a
photo, moved away, or took a moment

565
00:51:24,458 --> 00:51:29,346
before answering. Give yourself about
twenty seconds. Then, if you’re with

566
00:51:29,347 --> 00:51:34,435
colleagues, two or three people might
share one example. Sharing is optional.

567
00:51:34,436 --> 00:51:38,720
Pause the video now if you’d like that
thinking and sharing time.

568
00:51:46,721 --> 00:51:52,416
Let’s come back together. We move between
ways of communicating all day, often

569
00:51:52,417 --> 00:51:56,928
without noticing. Writing, facial
expression, gesture, sign, a

570
00:51:56,929 --> 00:52:02,845
communication device and speech are all
real communication. One method isn’t more

571
00:52:02,846 --> 00:52:08,615
valid because it is spoken. That gives us
something useful to notice at school.

572
00:52:08,616 --> 00:52:13,941
When we say, ‘Use your words,’ are we
overlooking a message a student has

573
00:52:13,942 --> 00:52:19,636
already communicated? A point, a look, or
moving closer or further away can be

574
00:52:19,637 --> 00:52:25,628
worth attending to. Our job is to listen
across the ways that person communicates,

575
00:52:25,629 --> 00:52:29,769
while checking what a message means rather
than assuming.

576
00:52:31,280 --> 00:52:35,839
There are two parts to keep distinct here:
understanding and expressing.

577
00:52:35,840 --> 00:52:40,850
Understanding is information coming in and
making sense. That might be a spoken

578
00:52:40,851 --> 00:52:45,538
instruction, a written sequence, a
demonstration, or a gesture in context.

579
00:52:45,539 --> 00:52:50,484
Expressing is a message going out: an
idea, a feeling, a question or a choice,

580
00:52:50,485 --> 00:52:55,173
through any communication method. At
school, a primary student may receive

581
00:52:55,174 --> 00:52:59,734
much of this information from one teacher,
while a secondary student may

582
00:52:59,735 --> 00:53:04,744
receive it from several teachers across
the day. The forms and expectations can

583
00:53:04,745 --> 00:53:09,625
change between lessons. These don’t
necessarily look the same for one person.

584
00:53:09,626 --> 00:53:14,122
Someone may speak at length and still need
support to understand spoken

585
00:53:14,123 --> 00:53:19,260
information. Someone else may understand
much more than they can readily express.

586
00:53:19,261 --> 00:53:24,463
Neither fluent speech nor limited speech
gives us the whole picture. Communication

587
00:53:24,464 --> 00:53:29,152
can also change with the person, the
place, the demands and how someone is

588
00:53:29,153 --> 00:53:34,162
feeling. A A C means augmentative and
alternative communication. It can include

589
00:53:34,163 --> 00:53:39,300
gesture, signs, a communication board or
book, typing, or a communication device.

590
00:53:39,301 --> 00:53:44,054
Some people use it alongside speech and
some instead of speech. Repeated or

591
00:53:44,055 --> 00:53:48,678
scripted words can carry meaning too.
Listen for the context and possible

592
00:53:48,679 --> 00:53:52,532
purpose before correcting the form. We
don’t need to assign a

593
00:53:52,533 --> 00:53:57,157
language-processing label before
respecting a student’s communication. If

594
00:53:57,158 --> 00:54:01,653
we need an individual assessment, we
involve the relevant team. For our

595
00:54:01,654 --> 00:54:06,727
classroom practice, the question is: what
does this environment help the student

596
00:54:06,728 --> 00:54:10,196
understand, and what ways does it give
them to respond?

597
00:54:11,720 --> 00:54:15,960
We’re going to hear Autistic people
describe communication in their own

598
00:54:15,960 --> 00:54:20,564
words. As you watch, notice what supports
communication. Choose one idea that

599
00:54:20,564 --> 00:54:25,107
stands out to you. Let the speakers’
experiences lead your thinking, without

600
00:54:25,107 --> 00:54:28,438
expecting one account to describe every
Autistic person.

601
01:00:00,920 --> 01:00:05,420
What stood out to you about what supported
communication? Take a moment to

602
01:00:05,421 --> 01:00:10,414
choose one idea. You might think about a
communication strength, something another

603
01:00:10,415 --> 01:00:14,668
person did that helped, or the difference
the environment or available

604
01:00:14,669 --> 01:00:19,292
communication method made. If you’re
watching with colleagues, invite one or

605
01:00:19,293 --> 01:00:22,560
two brief responses. Pause here for that
conversation.

606
01:00:30,560 --> 01:00:35,714
As we come back, hold onto that specific
idea. There is no single Autistic

607
01:00:35,715 --> 01:00:40,728
communication profile, so we need to
listen to what helps the particular

608
01:00:40,729 --> 01:00:46,094
person. One practical point to carry into
school is availability. A student’s

609
01:00:46,095 --> 01:00:51,249
communication system needs to be where
communication happens: in class, at

610
01:00:51,250 --> 01:00:56,545
break, during transitions and when plans
change. It cannot be limited to one

611
01:00:56,546 --> 01:01:01,983
activity or a therapy session. We support
the student’s ways of communicating,

612
01:01:01,984 --> 01:01:06,926
working with the student, their family and
the relevant team. We aren’t

613
01:01:06,927 --> 01:01:10,032
prescribing a system from a workshop
example.

614
01:01:11,561 --> 01:01:16,775
Turn to page eleven of your Participant
Guide, or use a sheet of paper. Put one

615
01:01:16,776 --> 01:01:21,856
of the students you have in mind in the
centre. Around them are home, school,

616
01:01:21,857 --> 01:01:26,871
friends, community and supports. Who is
part of this student’s communication

617
01:01:26,872 --> 01:01:31,684
network? Add just one person or place now.
Use roles rather than names: a

618
01:01:31,685 --> 01:01:36,966
family member, a classmate, a teacher,
someone at a club. You can write, draw or

619
01:01:36,967 --> 01:01:41,981
use dot points. If it helps, ask a
colleague to scribe your ideas. You don’t

620
01:01:41,982 --> 01:01:47,263
need to fill every space. Networks can be
small or changing, and an empty box is

621
01:01:47,264 --> 01:01:52,678
fine. Give yourself about a minute and a
half. Pause the video while you make that

622
01:01:52,679 --> 01:01:53,680
first addition.

623
01:02:01,681 --> 01:02:06,503
Keep that map open. You can return to the
rest later. Different people and places

624
01:02:06,504 --> 01:02:10,964
may reveal different parts of a student’s
communication. A family member, a

625
01:02:10,965 --> 01:02:15,726
classroom teacher and a support worker may
each notice something valuable. We’re

626
01:02:15,727 --> 01:02:20,549
not deciding who understands the student
best. We’re recognising that the picture

627
01:02:20,550 --> 01:02:25,190
is shared. Where we know little about part
of that network, we have a question

628
01:02:25,191 --> 01:02:29,531
to explore with the student and family,
rather than a gap to fill with an

629
01:02:29,532 --> 01:02:30,193
assumption.

630
01:02:31,721 --> 01:02:36,717
Now let’s think about what should travel
between those people and places, so the

631
01:02:36,718 --> 01:02:41,334
student doesn’t have to start again with
every new person. We’ll use three

632
01:02:41,335 --> 01:02:45,889
colours. Purple is a communication
strength. Blue is something that helps

633
01:02:45,890 --> 01:02:50,569
communication. Orange is something you
would like others to know. Write one

634
01:02:50,570 --> 01:02:55,440
note, two, or all three. You can use one
of your students, without identifying

635
01:02:55,441 --> 01:03:00,247
them, or a made-up example. If you don’t
have coloured notes, label the three

636
01:03:00,248 --> 01:03:05,118
categories on paper. This is also on page
eleven. Allow about three minutes to

637
01:03:05,119 --> 01:03:10,115
write. Then, if you’re together, spend a
few minutes sharing. You can add a note

638
01:03:10,116 --> 01:03:15,175
to the board or read one out, and you can
keep your notes private. Leave any note

639
01:03:15,176 --> 01:03:19,919
with identifying details off the shared
board. Pause now for the writing and

640
01:03:19,920 --> 01:03:20,991
optional sharing.

641
01:03:29,000 --> 01:03:34,253
Let’s bring our attention back. Look
across the ideas available to you. What

642
01:03:34,254 --> 01:03:39,156
similarities do you notice? What strengths
might be missed if we looked

643
01:03:39,157 --> 01:03:44,760
only for speech? Which supports involve
changing what other people do or provide?

644
01:03:44,761 --> 01:03:50,153
Think about what would help a relief
teacher, a specialist or subject teacher,

645
01:03:50,154 --> 01:03:55,687
or staff on duty at break times. Useful
knowledge can travel between classrooms,

646
01:03:55,688 --> 01:04:00,940
subjects, break times and home, with the
student’s and family’s perspectives

647
01:04:00,941 --> 01:04:06,614
included. It can make continuity possible
when the people around a student change.

648
01:04:06,615 --> 01:04:12,078
Keep the blue notes in view: the things
that help communication. We’re going to

649
01:04:12,079 --> 01:04:15,439
connect those ideas with three practical
options.

650
01:04:16,961 --> 01:04:22,530
The first option is clear communication.
Use familiar, specific words and give one

651
01:04:22,531 --> 01:04:27,824
idea or step at a time, in a form and
amount the person can access. That might

652
01:04:27,825 --> 01:04:33,188
mean making a direction shorter or making
the first step clearer. The second is

653
01:04:33,189 --> 01:04:37,589
processing time. After giving information,
pause. A person may be

654
01:04:37,590 --> 01:04:43,021
making sense of what you said, organising
a response and communicating it. If we

655
01:04:43,022 --> 01:04:48,453
fill every pause with a new version of the
question, we may add more information

656
01:04:48,454 --> 01:04:53,816
before the earlier information has been
processed. The third is visual support:

657
01:04:53,817 --> 01:04:59,455
showing information as well as saying it,
so there is something to return to. These

658
01:04:59,456 --> 01:05:04,818
are options to match to the person and the
moment, rather than a checklist that

659
01:05:04,819 --> 01:05:10,319
guarantees success. Keep the student’s A A
C available and accept their preferred

660
01:05:10,320 --> 01:05:15,407
ways of responding, including refusal. A
useful communication support makes

661
01:05:15,408 --> 01:05:20,977
messages easier to understand and share.
It doesn’t require the student to produce

662
01:05:20,978 --> 01:05:24,482
the answer or communication style we were
expecting.

663
01:05:26,000 --> 01:05:31,134
Visual supports are information you can
see and come back to. The three examples

664
01:05:31,135 --> 01:05:35,879
here answer different questions. A timer
can show how much time is left. A

665
01:05:35,880 --> 01:05:40,884
sequence can show what to do next. A daily
schedule can show what is happening

666
01:05:40,885 --> 01:05:46,149
today. You may already use a calendar, a
shopping list or a reminder on your phone

667
01:05:46,150 --> 01:05:51,024
in much the same way. The format needs to
make sense to the person. Objects,

668
01:05:51,025 --> 01:05:55,834
photographs, written words and symbols can
all be options, on paper or on a

669
01:05:55,835 --> 01:05:59,864
device. Check that the student understands
what the information

670
01:05:59,865 --> 01:06:04,803
represents and can reach it when it is
needed. A support displayed across the

671
01:06:04,804 --> 01:06:09,808
room may exist without being useful at the
moment of need. Think of the visual

672
01:06:09,809 --> 01:06:14,813
information already available in your
classroom, or something you have seen on

673
01:06:14,814 --> 01:06:19,753
placement. What question does it answer?
Could a student return to it without

674
01:06:19,754 --> 01:06:24,758
asking an adult? A visual support offers
information; it isn’t a condition the

675
01:06:24,759 --> 01:06:30,023
student must satisfy to access something
they enjoy. Visual supports and A A C can

676
01:06:30,024 --> 01:06:35,223
overlap, but they aren’t interchangeable.
Keep the person’s communication methods

677
01:06:35,224 --> 01:06:40,293
available as well. We’ll return to this in
Session Four when you choose options

678
01:06:40,294 --> 01:06:42,438
for your day-one classroom setup.

679
01:06:43,961 --> 01:06:48,425
Double empathy asks us to look at
understanding in both directions. When

680
01:06:48,426 --> 01:06:53,205
people have different experiences and ways
of communicating, it can be harder

681
01:06:53,206 --> 01:06:57,293
for them to understand one another. That
doesn’t mean every social

682
01:06:57,294 --> 01:07:02,450
misunderstanding sits inside the Autistic
student. Consider a classroom discussion,

683
01:07:02,451 --> 01:07:06,978
group task or playground interaction.
Whose expectations are shaping what

684
01:07:06,979 --> 01:07:12,009
counts as joining in? Who is expected to
adjust? What would make this interaction

685
01:07:12,010 --> 01:07:16,852
clearer or safer for the people involved?
For example, an adult might expect a

686
01:07:16,853 --> 01:07:21,191
quick spoken response as a sign of
connection. A student might connect

687
01:07:21,192 --> 01:07:25,845
through a shared activity, through
detailed talk about an interest, or with

688
01:07:25,846 --> 01:07:30,247
more time between turns. Those are
possible differences to explore, not

689
01:07:30,248 --> 01:07:35,027
conclusions to impose on a particular
child. The question is how each student

690
01:07:35,028 --> 01:07:40,184
connects. Connection doesn’t need to look
one way. Our expectations and the context

691
01:07:40,185 --> 01:07:44,964
shape the interaction too, so we have
something useful to examine and change.

692
01:07:44,965 --> 01:07:49,932
We can make room for different ways of
being together while continuing to listen

693
01:07:49,933 --> 01:07:52,132
to what each person actually wants.

694
01:07:53,641 --> 01:07:59,205
Let’s carry that idea into the many ways
students connect. As we watch this next

695
01:07:59,206 --> 01:08:04,419
clip, keep the question open: how does
each student connect? Notice people,

696
01:08:04,420 --> 01:08:09,843
places, activities and ways of joining. A
different style of connection is not

697
01:08:09,844 --> 01:08:12,308
evidence that connection is absent.

698
01:08:57,401 --> 01:09:01,616
Keep those possibilities in mind as we
widen the picture beyond a single

699
01:09:01,617 --> 01:09:06,247
interaction. We can look for connection in
a student’s preferred activities and

700
01:09:06,248 --> 01:09:10,402
relationships, while allowing their own
ways of showing it to guide us.

701
01:09:11,921 --> 01:09:16,203
A student’s social world can include
family, school, community and online

702
01:09:16,204 --> 01:09:20,724
spaces. Connection might be one to one,
playing or working alongside someone,

703
01:09:20,725 --> 01:09:25,186
sharing a great deal about an interest, or
seeing someone only occasionally.

704
01:09:25,187 --> 01:09:29,886
Being together quietly can matter as much
as a lively conversation. Culture also

705
01:09:29,887 --> 01:09:34,466
shapes expectations about interaction and
relationships. We need to be careful

706
01:09:34,467 --> 01:09:38,868
about treating the pattern most familiar
to us as the measure of meaningful

707
01:09:38,869 --> 01:09:43,330
connection for everyone. There may be
spaces outside home and school where a

708
01:09:43,331 --> 01:09:47,792
student feels a particular sense of
belonging: a club, a community activity,

709
01:09:47,793 --> 01:09:52,432
or another shared-interest space. There
may be online connections too. We don’t

710
01:09:52,433 --> 01:09:56,358
need to disclose a student’s online
activity, identity or community

711
01:09:56,359 --> 01:10:00,582
membership to recognise that these
possibilities exist. Keep the picture

712
01:10:00,583 --> 01:10:05,103
individual. What do we know about this
student’s preferred people, places and

713
01:10:05,104 --> 01:10:09,505
ways of joining? What are we assuming?
What could we find out respectfully?

714
01:10:11,040 --> 01:10:15,514
Find a partner for a conversation about
connection. The questions are on page

715
01:10:15,515 --> 01:10:20,047
twelve of your Participant Guide. First,
when do the students you have in mind

716
01:10:20,048 --> 01:10:24,522
seem most comfortable connecting with
others at school? Second, what kinds of

717
01:10:24,523 --> 01:10:29,173
connection does each enjoy? That might be
talking, gaming, sport, making things,

718
01:10:29,174 --> 01:10:33,353
shared interests, or being together
quietly. Third, are there times when

719
01:10:33,354 --> 01:10:37,827
being social seems enjoyable, and times
when it seems tiring or overwhelming?

720
01:10:37,828 --> 01:10:42,302
Finally, what could educators change to
make connection feel safer, easier or

721
01:10:42,303 --> 01:10:46,541
more meaningful? Use about four minutes.
You don’t need to cover all four

722
01:10:46,542 --> 01:10:51,016
questions. Keep identifying details
private, and hold anything you don’t know

723
01:10:51,017 --> 01:10:55,078
as a question. You can reflect
individually if you’re watching on your

724
01:10:55,079 --> 01:10:57,432
own. Pause the video for the discussion.

725
01:11:05,441 --> 01:11:10,036
Let’s come back to the last question: what
could educators change around the

726
01:11:10,037 --> 01:11:14,755
student? If you’re together, you might
offer one practical possibility without

727
01:11:14,756 --> 01:11:19,289
describing the student. Notice whether
your idea makes room for the kind of

728
01:11:19,290 --> 01:11:23,885
connection the student values. Does it
change an expectation, an activity, a

729
01:11:23,886 --> 01:11:28,849
space or the pace of interaction? Does it
leave room for choosing when to join and

730
01:11:28,850 --> 01:11:33,813
when to step back? The aim is meaningful
connection for that person. We don’t need

731
01:11:33,814 --> 01:11:36,508
to make everyone look social in the same
way.

732
01:11:38,041 --> 01:11:43,678
Now we’ll look at daily capacity.
Communication and connection are part of

733
01:11:43,679 --> 01:11:48,930
a much larger day, and that day asks
different amounts of energy from

734
01:11:48,931 --> 01:11:54,646
different students. As we move into the
next activities, we’ll use a simple

735
01:11:54,647 --> 01:12:00,825
question: what costs, and what lightens?
That language will help us look beyond a

736
01:12:00,826 --> 01:12:06,849
single task to the pattern of demands and
opportunities for recovery across the

737
01:12:06,850 --> 01:12:07,157
day.

738
01:12:08,681 --> 01:12:13,477
Think of a student’s available energy as a
battery. Each day starts with some

739
01:12:13,478 --> 01:12:18,274
charge, and it isn’t necessarily the same
amount as yesterday. Activities and

740
01:12:18,275 --> 01:12:22,818
demands draw on that energy. As the
battery runs lower, managing the next

741
01:12:22,819 --> 01:12:27,552
demand can become harder. You may also
hear people in the Autistic community

742
01:12:27,553 --> 01:12:32,538
talk about spoons. The idea is having a
limited supply available: an action uses

743
01:12:32,539 --> 01:12:37,399
one spoon or several, and when spoons are
low, there is less left for the next

744
01:12:37,400 --> 01:12:42,195
thing. These are ways of talking about
capacity, rather than a measure we can

745
01:12:42,196 --> 01:12:46,929
calculate for someone else. The other part
of the picture is recharging. The

746
01:12:46,930 --> 01:12:51,916
right supports can reduce demand and help
restore some capacity. A pause, useful

747
01:12:51,917 --> 01:12:56,460
information, a preferred way to
communicate or a safe connection may make

748
01:12:56,461 --> 01:13:01,383
a difference, depending on the person. So
we’ll look for both sides in a school

749
01:13:01,384 --> 01:13:05,928
day: what draws on capacity, and what
helps to restore it. What helps one

750
01:13:05,929 --> 01:13:08,261
student may be different for another.

751
01:13:09,801 --> 01:13:14,789
Capacity fluctuates. Like all of us,
Autistic students can have easier days

752
01:13:14,790 --> 01:13:19,981
and harder days. Stress, energy, sensory
demands and what else is happening in

753
01:13:19,982 --> 01:13:25,307
life can change what is manageable. That
means the same student may do something

754
01:13:25,308 --> 01:13:30,701
one day and need more support with it the
next. Yesterday’s success doesn’t prove

755
01:13:30,702 --> 01:13:36,163
that today’s difficulty is a choice, and
finishing a task doesn’t tell us how much

756
01:13:36,164 --> 01:13:41,287
effort it required. Hold that in mind when
you look at the students you know.

757
01:13:41,288 --> 01:13:46,478
We can ask what is different today, what
demands have already accumulated, and

758
01:13:46,479 --> 01:13:51,670
what support is available now. We don’t
have to know every cause before making

759
01:13:51,671 --> 01:13:56,997
information clearer or allowing a useful
pause. We do need to keep checking with

760
01:13:56,998 --> 01:14:02,322
the student and the people who know them,
instead of treating capacity as fixed.

761
01:14:03,840 --> 01:14:08,771
Turn to page thirteen. We’re going to map
a typical day in your classroom from

762
01:14:08,772 --> 01:14:13,895
arrival to departure. If you don’t have
your own class yet, use a placement class

763
01:14:13,896 --> 01:14:18,827
or a class you know well. In secondary,
follow a student’s day across periods,

764
01:14:18,828 --> 01:14:23,887
rooms and teachers. Relief teachers can
draw on a recent booking or a class they

765
01:14:23,888 --> 01:14:28,242
return to often. Start with a simple
timeline. Then add the routines,

766
01:14:28,243 --> 01:14:32,854
transitions and changes that matter in
your setting. Follow the students’

767
01:14:32,855 --> 01:14:37,529
experience as well as the official
timetable. Where are the shifts between

768
01:14:37,530 --> 01:14:42,653
tasks, the waiting, the movement and the
breaks? Where can a student communicate,

769
01:14:42,654 --> 01:14:47,841
choose, pause or recover? Include sensory
and communication demands where you know

770
01:14:47,842 --> 01:14:52,965
they are relevant. Then mark how the same
moments may land for each of the two or

771
01:14:52,966 --> 01:14:57,641
three students you have in mind. You can
use different marks for different

772
01:14:57,642 --> 01:15:02,701
students, without names or identifying
incidents. If you don’t know how a moment

773
01:15:02,702 --> 01:15:07,633
is experienced, leave it as a question.
Leave some room on your map: we’ll add

774
01:15:07,634 --> 01:15:12,436
another layer next. Allow about six
minutes, with optional discussion with a

775
01:15:12,437 --> 01:15:17,368
partner. Keep the map private unless you
choose to discuss it. Pause the video

776
01:15:17,369 --> 01:15:18,329
while you work.

777
01:15:26,361 --> 01:15:31,267
Let’s pause our drawing there. You don’t
need a polished or complete map. This is

778
01:15:31,268 --> 01:15:35,990
a working picture to extend, rather than a
judgement of a student or a school.

779
01:15:35,991 --> 01:15:40,652
Look briefly at the same point in the day
for your different students. It may

780
01:15:40,653 --> 01:15:45,621
involve different communication, sensory
or task demands for each of them. That is

781
01:15:45,622 --> 01:15:50,528
why we’re keeping several real students in
view. Keep the map beside you; it will

782
01:15:50,529 --> 01:15:52,551
travel with us into Session Four.

783
01:15:54,080 --> 01:15:59,819
Now turn to page fourteen and return to
your day map. Use one mark or colour for

784
01:15:59,820 --> 01:16:04,469
what costs: what adds demand or
uncertainty. Use another for what

785
01:16:04,470 --> 01:16:10,282
lightens: what reduces demand, restores
capacity or brings joy. Think back to the

786
01:16:10,283 --> 01:16:15,586
battery image: what drains, and what might
recharge? Add questions where a

787
01:16:15,587 --> 01:16:20,091
student’s experience isn’t known.
Something can be enjoyable or

788
01:16:20,092 --> 01:16:25,613
successfully completed and still take
considerable effort. We can record what

789
01:16:25,614 --> 01:16:31,136
we observe, but we can’t read an internal
state from a single moment. Include

790
01:16:31,137 --> 01:16:36,295
strengths, joy, safe connection and
belonging. We want a picture of what

791
01:16:36,296 --> 01:16:41,526
sustains a student as well as what is
difficult. Give yourself about four

792
01:16:41,527 --> 01:16:45,813
minutes to add this layer, and pause the
video while you do.

793
01:16:53,840 --> 01:16:58,559
Come back to the pattern across the day.
Where do several demands occur close

794
01:16:58,560 --> 01:17:03,278
together? Where are the opportunities for
relief or recovery? What is already

795
01:17:03,279 --> 01:17:08,309
working well and worth protecting? Treat
these marks as prompts for inquiry, not a

796
01:17:08,310 --> 01:17:12,904
score. What costs or lightens can differ
between students, and for the same

797
01:17:12,905 --> 01:17:17,438
student on different days. Choose one
pattern you would like to understand

798
01:17:17,439 --> 01:17:22,157
better. Keep that question on your day
map. We will use it in Session Four to

799
01:17:22,158 --> 01:17:25,511
consider what could be ready for students
from day one.

800
01:17:27,041 --> 01:17:32,071
This school-day stack gives us another way
to see accumulation. The darker bars

801
01:17:32,072 --> 01:17:36,845
represent demand. The lighter bars
represent relief and recovery. They’re a

802
01:17:36,846 --> 01:17:41,940
visual cue, rather than quantities we can
assign to a child. A moment that looks

803
01:17:41,941 --> 01:17:46,972
small by itself may arrive after many
other demands. The last transition of the

804
01:17:46,973 --> 01:17:51,358
day sits within everything that came
before it. Equally, a supportive

805
01:17:51,359 --> 01:17:56,389
interaction or an opportunity to recover
belongs in the picture too. So when we

806
01:17:56,390 --> 01:18:01,227
notice a difficult moment, we can ask what
the day has already asked of this

807
01:18:01,228 --> 01:18:05,871
student and what support has been
available. Keep this qualitative. We’re

808
01:18:05,872 --> 01:18:10,773
looking for patterns and questions that
help us improve access, while keeping

809
01:18:10,774 --> 01:18:13,803
relief, recovery and joy visible alongside
cost.

810
01:18:15,321 --> 01:18:19,810
There are other things that can draw on
our batteries or spoons, including effort

811
01:18:19,811 --> 01:18:24,244
that isn’t obvious to someone watching.
Let’s hear the next video about masking.

812
01:18:24,245 --> 01:18:28,677
Keep in mind the difference between how a
person appears on the outside and what

813
01:18:28,678 --> 01:18:29,855
they may be managing.

814
01:20:00,281 --> 01:20:05,868
As we return from the video, keep that
distinction open. Visible behaviour gives

815
01:20:05,869 --> 01:20:11,032
us part of the picture. We’ll now connect
this with identity, capacity and

816
01:20:11,033 --> 01:20:12,447
belonging at school.

817
01:20:13,961 --> 01:20:19,191
A student can appear fine without feeling
fine. Quietness, compliance or completed

818
01:20:19,192 --> 01:20:24,229
work doesn’t give us a full account of
what someone is managing. Masking brings

819
01:20:24,230 --> 01:20:28,749
that question into our thinking about
identity and belonging. Hold four

820
01:20:28,750 --> 01:20:33,270
questions in mind. What might this student
be working hard to manage or

821
01:20:33,271 --> 01:20:38,372
hide? Where can they be recognised and
included? What supports belonging without

822
01:20:38,373 --> 01:20:43,474
requiring them to appear less Autistic?
And what does the student want others to

823
01:20:43,475 --> 01:20:47,995
understand? We can’t answer those
questions simply by watching from the

824
01:20:47,996 --> 01:20:52,709
outside. They invite us to listen and to
examine the conditions around the

825
01:20:52,710 --> 01:20:57,553
student. Include what feels safe and
joyful, the connections they value, and

826
01:20:57,554 --> 01:21:02,849
places where they can be themselves. This
is a reason to look beyond whether a task

827
01:21:02,850 --> 01:21:07,434
was finished or a lesson was quiet. Access
matters, and so do the effort

828
01:21:07,435 --> 01:21:10,662
involved and the student’s experience of
belonging.

829
01:21:12,201 --> 01:21:17,069
Return to page twelve for the masking
discussion. With a partner, consider

830
01:21:17,070 --> 01:21:22,338
three questions. When might one of the
students you have in mind be working hard

831
01:21:22,339 --> 01:21:27,340
to appear fine at school when they are
not? When and where do they seem most

832
01:21:27,341 --> 01:21:32,142
comfortable being themselves? What could
we change to help them feel more

833
01:21:32,143 --> 01:21:37,477
comfortable and supported? You have about
three minutes. Keep examples anonymised

834
01:21:37,478 --> 01:21:42,680
and distinguish what you know from what
you wonder. Avoid assuming that a quiet

835
01:21:42,681 --> 01:21:47,348
student is coping, or that outward
behaviour tells you the whole story.

836
01:21:47,349 --> 01:21:49,816
Pause the video for the conversation.

837
01:21:57,841 --> 01:22:03,438
Let’s come back together. Focus on one
possible change, rather than a conclusion

838
01:22:03,439 --> 01:22:09,178
about what a student must be feeling. If
you’re sharing with colleagues, offer the

839
01:22:09,179 --> 01:22:14,705
change without identifying the child.
Perhaps your next step is to ask a better

840
01:22:14,706 --> 01:22:19,949
question, understand an existing source of
comfort, or change a demand in a

